  title="How this journal is prepared"
  eyebrow="OUR EDITORIAL APPROACH"
  description="Understand the distinction between authorship, AI assistance and medical review in The Women’s Health Journal."
>
  <div class="prose editorial-policy">
    <h2>Clear information starts with clear attribution.</h2>
    <p>
      Each article identifies its actual author or editorial publisher, medical
      reviewer and recorded publication and review dates. Medical review is a
      separate role from writing.
    </p>
    <h2>AI assistance, disclosed.</h2>
    <p>
      Where AI assists editorial preparation, that assistance is labelled.
      AI-assisted articles are not presented as personally written by the doctor
      unless genuine authorship is established.
    </p>
    <h2>Reviewed before publication.</h2>
    <p>
      Medical content follows a draft, medical review, approval and publication
      process. References, clinical explanations and the scope of the review are
      considered before a page is published. The review date shown is a recorded
      review event, not the date you visit the page.
    </p>
    <h2>Sources and updates.</h2>
    <p>
      Sources accompany each article. Guidance and individual circumstances can
      differ. A published date, a substantive update and a medical review are
      recorded separately; a visual design change does not create a new medical
      review date.
    </p>
    <h2>Information for a conversation.</h2>
    <p>
      The journal offers general educational information. It cannot diagnose
      your circumstances or replace individual medical advice. Appointment
      requests are not intended for emergencies.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/blog/">Explore the journal</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/blog/authors/dr-vasundara/">Meet the medical reviewer</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Page>
